        "content": "\n1. **No test runner installed.** Add `vitest`, `@vitejs/plugin-react`, `jsdom`, `@testing-library/react`, `@testing-library/dom`, `vite-tsconfig-paths`.\n2. **`import \"server-only\"` will throw in a Vitest/Node/jsdom environment.** Every file in `lib/server/*` and `app/api/**/route.ts` imports it. Needed in `vitest.config.mts`:\n   ```ts\n   import { defineConfig } from \"vitest/config\";\n   import react from \"@vitejs/plugin-react\";\n   import tsconfigPaths from \"vite-tsconfig-paths\";\n\n   export default defineConfig({\n     plugins: [tsconfigPaths(), react()],\n     test: {\n       environment: \"jsdom\",\n       alias: { \"server-only\": new URL(\"./test/server-only-stub.ts\", import.meta.url).pathname },\n     },\n   });\n   ```\n   (`test/server-only-stub.ts` → empty file.)\n3. **No DB test strategy.** `lib/server/{audit,conflicts,rulesets,runs,sources,entities}.ts` call `getDb()` directly — none of it is injectable. Two real options: (a) point `DATABASE_URL` at a disposable Postgres (docker/testcontainers) and run real queries in integration tests, or (b) refactor these modules to accept a `db` param for unit testing. Nothing works today without one of these.\n4. **No route-handler test convention.** App Router `route.ts` exports (`GET`/`POST`/`PATCH`) are plain functions and can be unit-tested by calling them with a `Request` — but every route calls `requireReviewer()` (Clerk) and the server modules above, so they need `vi.mock(...)` for `@clerk/nextjs/server` and the `lib/server/*` functions they import.\n\n---\n\n",
